    A parked association finds or initializes, and a helper's read of it queries
    
    Since once-campfire c8f74a0 the push-subscriptions controller opens with
    `@push_subscriptions.find_or_initialize_by(push_subscription_params)`
    on the association its `before_action` parks, and looks the endpoint up
    again in the private `register_while_signed_in` (`@push_subscriptions
    .find_by(...)`, under the write lock). Three things kept that off a
    relation:
    
    * `find_or_initialize_by` was not a relation terminal beside
      `find_or_create_by`, so the parked read stayed the reader's Array.
      `Relation#find_or_initialize_by` already gives the new record the
      scope's attributes, as Rails' does through an association.
    * The params object reached the conditions as itself, and the relation
      wrote `#<PushSubscriptionParams…>` into the SQL (`unrecognized token:
      "#"`). The find-or-build/create family joins `find_by` among the
      runtime slots that take the condition hash (`.to_attrs`).
    * A method that READS an ivar without assigning it never saw the
      binding an action recorded. An ivar every method of the class binds
      to one and the same association read (compared by emitted text, so an
      inlined `before_action` agrees with itself) is now recorded up front
      for the methods that only read it. In Rails it is the same proxy.
    
    Campfire's suite at c8f74a0: 589 -> 598 of 630 tests, 78 -> 80 of 89
    files (both push-subscription files pass). At the pinned 11d3fb9 it is
    unchanged at 578/584 and 82/86.
    
    tests/emit_and_run.rs a_parked_association_finds_or_initializes_and_a_
    helper_reads_it: a comment posted twice is one row on its own article,
    another article is another scope, and a private helper's find_by of the
    parked ivar answers; it fails with any one of the three changes removed.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <[redacted-email]>

